<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> (skill)

The <ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill governs your ability to find hidden
things: secret exits, concealed containers, stashed items, hidden creatures,
and obscured objects. It also powers your capacity to track creatures and
forage for materials.

Searching relies on your <ansi fg="yellow">Perception</ansi> stat and your
<ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill rank. The keener your eye and the more
skilled you are, the more you can uncover.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The search Command ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">search</ansi>          Search the whole room.
  <ansi fg="command">search [thing]</ansi>  Search one thing in the room, carefully.

<ansi fg="command">look</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">search</ansi> are the two ways you deal with the things in a room. Use
<ansi fg="command">look</ansi> to learn more about something the description mentions (see
<ansi fg="command">help look</ansi>). Use <ansi fg="command">search</ansi> when something looks disturbed, out of place,
or like it could be hiding something.

<ansi fg="command">search [thing]</ansi> searches that one thing and nothing else. It is the
better choice when a room points at something, such as a loose stone, a
shifted bookshelf or a disturbed patch of ground. Try
<ansi fg="command">search bookshelf</ansi> or <ansi fg="command">search under the table</ansi>.

A plain <ansi fg="command">search</ansi> goes over the whole room. It is slower to pay off and
less certain. It can turn up the same secrets as a careful search of one
thing, and it can also find what only a whole-room search can: hidden
exits, stashes, and creatures that are hiding.

Search someone's home while they are about, and what you turn up stays
where you found it: it is theirs. Taking it is stealing, with
<ansi fg="command">steal</ansi> (see <ansi fg="command">help steal</ansi>). Things left lying untouched are gone within a day.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Recovery Time ━━━</ansi>

Searching takes the same recovery time (cooldown) as special moves and
spells. You cannot search and then use a special move or cast a spell
straight away, or the other way round. Wait for the cooldown to end first.
Type <ansi fg="command">cooldowns</ansi> to check (see <ansi fg="command">help cooldowns</ansi>).

As your <ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill improves through repeated use, you may
discover things you previously missed.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Search Applications ━━━</ansi>

Your <ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill opens doors to three distinct arts:

  <ansi fg="command">track</ansi>  Follow trails left by creatures and players.
            Higher search skill lets you follow trails farther and read
            more detail from the tracks you find.

  <ansi fg="command">forage</ansi> Gather herbs, wood, ore, and materials from
            natural environments. Your search skill determines what
            quality materials you can uncover, and how rare the finds
            might be.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Skill Progression ━━━</ansi>

The <ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill improves through use. Every search
attempt has a small chance to deepen your mastery. Over time, your
<ansi fg="yellow">Perception</ansi> and your accumulated rank in
<ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> will determine what you can find.

